# Summary
Introduced: the commit replaces the previous stale-epoch error during heartbeat with deletion of epoch-mismatched pending slots. The deletion condition does not prove the slot is orphaned; absence of a terminal log entry is also true for legitimate in-flight commands.
The new heartbeat path treats any pending metadata command whose stored epoch does not equal the replica state's current durable epoch as an orphan if there is not yet a terminal log entry, and deletes it. However, a normal first metadata command in a newer cluster epoch has exactly this shape while it is in flight: the pending slot is written for the new epoch before apply/record advances metadata_command_replica_state to that epoch. Heartbeat cleanup is not serialized with the higher-level metadata command install/apply critical section, so it can race with a legitimate command between pending-slot insertion and terminal-log recording. If the slot is removed during that window, recovery loses the pending command, a later command may reuse the same next log index, and a crash or partial apply can leave metadata replicas diverged or operations lost.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [8bcda43f97e85bc3ad7546b5046a40dd4516251f](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/8bcda43f97e85bc3ad7546b5046a40dd4516251f) (`Keep heartbeat pending-slot observation read-only`).

Heartbeat is now a read-only observation boundary for pending metadata command slots. `SharedStorageNode::pg_heartbeat_observation` reports the durable metadata proof and whether any pending slot exists, but it no longer deletes epoch-mismatched pending slots, cleans terminal same-epoch slots, or runs replay validation that can mutate pending-slot state.

Pending-slot reconciliation is now explicitly recovery-scoped. `PgStoreRecoveryContext` is an opaque constructor-backed token, and epoch-mismatched orphan cleanup requires that recovery context and derives the stored epoch internally. This makes the type boundary reflect the invariant: pre-serving recovery may repair crash leftovers; heartbeat and other serving-time observation paths must not mutate pending slots.

The storage invariant guide was updated to document this recovery-vs-heartbeat split. Regression coverage now proves heartbeat preserves both epoch-mismatched and terminal pending slots, while recovery still cleans epoch-mismatched orphan slots and terminal pending slots before serving.

