## Report
Validated the finding on commit 791b409c. The heartbeat path reads the durable metadata epoch, then reads a pending slot from any epoch, and if the slot epoch differs it calls cleanup and reports no pending command: crates/storage/src/node.rs:1200-1211. The cleanup function only checks whether a terminal log entry exists; if none exists, it removes the pending slot: crates/storage/src/pg_store/command_log.rs:2049-2077. Metadata command installation writes the pending slot before later apply/record: crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:3918-3950. Apply/record happens later under the metadata-command path: crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:1117-1190, and durable replica state is advanced during log-state update/recording in crates/storage/src/pg_store/command_log.rs:3679-3755. I added a targeted Rust unit-test PoC that creates the in-flight shape: durable metadata replica epoch is ClusterEpoch::INITIAL, then a valid pending command slot is inserted for epoch 2. Calling SharedStorageNode::pg_heartbeat_observation deletes that slot. The reproducer fails with: `after heartbeat: has_pending_metadata_command=false, slot_after_present=false` followed by panic `heartbeat lost active future-epoch pending slot`. This demonstrates the cleanup predicate cannot distinguish an orphan from an active future-epoch command before apply/record. I also attempted valgrind/gdb; both were unavailable in the container. LLDB was available and confirmed at the failing assertion that `observation.has_pending_metadata_command = false` and `slot_after = None`.

